<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isthmus of Corinth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Isthmus of Corinth is the narrow strip of land, about 6 kilometres wide at its narrowest, that joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> to mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Long fortified against invaders, notably by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hexamilion-wall">Hexamilion wall</a>, it was crossed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinth-canal">Corinth Canal</a>, completed in 1893.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth.jpg" alt="Isthmus of Corinth" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philos2000 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kanal-Korinth-2011.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The isthmus lies near the city of Corinth, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-corinth">Gulf of Corinth</a> to the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saronic-gulf">Saronic Gulf</a> to the east, and is 6.3 kilometres wide. In antiquity it was the landmark that separated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> from mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. The geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, in the first century CE, recorded a stele on the isthmus with two inscriptions: the one facing east towards Megara read &quot;Here is not Peloponnesus, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>&quot;, and the one facing west read &quot;Here is Peloponnesus, not Ionia&quot;. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> ascribed its erection to the Attic hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a>, on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> long considered a shortcut that would spare ships the voyage around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. The tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periander">Periander</a> tried to cut a canal in the 7th century BCE but gave it up because of technical difficulties, and built instead a stone ramp, the Diolkos, along which ships were dragged overland. Remains of the Diolkos survive beside the modern canal. Under Roman rule, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> saw the value of a link for his new colony, Colonia Laus Iulia Corinthiensis.</p><p>Engineers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> tried to dig a canal but lacked the equipment, and boats were rolled across on logs instead, a method in use by 32 CE. In 67 CE the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> ordered 6,000 slaves to dig a canal with spades; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Flavius Josephus</a> says they were Jewish captives taken by Vespasian. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> the work advanced about 0.8 kilometres. Nero died the next year and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galba">Galba</a> abandoned the project as too costly.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> also built the Hexamilion, a defensive wall across the isthmus that guarded the only land route into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> and is the largest single archaeological structure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The idea of a canal was seriously proposed again in 1830, soon after Greek independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinth-canal">Corinth Canal</a> was completed in 1893 after eleven years' work. It effectively makes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> an island, and road, railway and submersible bridges cross it. Campaigners call for better protection of the Diolkos.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isthmus of Corinth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Beyond the Aegean, Megara left the Peloponnesian League to ally with Athens, and a double line of walls was built across the <b>Isthmus of Corinth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The court at Andravida was famous for its French culture: the Venetian Marino Sanudo Torsello wrote that French was spoken there as well as in Paris, and tournaments, such as the one held at the <b>Isthmus of Corinth</b> in 1305, were part of aristocratic life.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In autumn 481 BCE, as the Persian king Xerxes gathered his army in Asia Minor, delegates of the Greek states willing to resist met at the <b>Isthmus of Corinth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hellenic-alliance">Hellenic Alliance</a></li></ul></section>
